target_papers.conclusion:
In this work, we proposed \modelname, the first defense against LLM jailbreak attacks based on the IB principle.
The design allows a tractable optimization to produce compacted and label-preserving sub-prompts.
Our method is lightweight and does not require modifications for expensive retraining or fine-tuning of the target LLM, and its trained extractors have transferable attack-mitigation ability.
We further give a solution for which gradient search is not available in \modelname.
Relevant comparative studies confirm that \modelname~surpasses existing defense methods without substantially affecting the LLM's ability to reply and reasoning speed.
A potential issue is that extracted information only highlights the most harmful parts as intermediate results that are difficult for humans to understand.
Hence, it will be interesting to explore generating fluent sub-prompts.




