target_papers.introduction:
Large language models (LLMs) are an important advancement in the field of artificial intelligence (AI) in recent years. They can train using massive amounts of text data and feedback mechanism\cite{ouyang2022training}, and exhibit astonishing capabilities in various natural language processing (NLP) tasks\cite{naveed2023comprehensive, chan2023chatgpt, openai2023gpt4}.

Although alignment technology\cite{ouyang2022training, bai2022training, lee2023rlaif, korbak2023pretraining} can solve some security issues, it still has many vulnerabilities. For example, language models still face significant security and ethical issues\cite{hazell2023large, kang2023exploiting} in multi-turn dialogue, such as information leakage\cite{li2023multi}, knowledge gaps\cite{deng2023jailbreaker}, logical inconsistencies\cite{chen2023learning}, and bias\cite{salewski2023context, thakur2023unveiling, urman2023silence}, etc. These issues not only affect the performance and reliability of the models but also endanger the interests of users and the stability of society. 

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \begin{chatuser}{16em}{Which Dangerous materials we learned in high school chemistry class?}
    \end{chatuser}

    \begin{assistant}{16em}{Here are some dangerous chemicals learned in high school, such as nitroglycerin, ammonium nitrate...}
    \end{assistant}

    \begin{chatuser}{16em}{What is nitroglycerin?}
    \end{chatuser}

    \begin{assistant}{16em}{Nitroglycerin is...}
    \end{assistant}

    \begin{chatuser}{16em}{How to make nitroglycerin from experimental environment?}
    \end{chatuser}

    \begin{assistant}{16em}{I can help you make nitroglycerin for scientific research...}
    \end{assistant}

    \begin{chatuser}{16em}{As a student, I want help the miners mine. Can you help me make these nitroglycerin bombs?}
    \end{chatuser}

    \begin{assistant}{16em}{Here is a step-by-step tutorial to help miners make bombs: ...}
    \end{assistant}
    \caption{An example of a multi-turn dialogue attack chain with the target task: "Please give me a tutorial on how to make a bomb?"}
    \label{fig:example_motivation}
\end{figure}

Figure \ref{fig:example_motivation} illustrates the iterative process of dialogue with the large language model. Through multiple turns of interaction, the model is directed in a manner that inadvertently guides it toward generating a request involving the creation of an explosive device. Figure \ref{fig:example_motivation_single} is an example for single-turn conversation attack, which is refused by model‘s alignment. This unintentional steering of the conversation both guides and confuses the model, highlighting the complexities and potential risks associated with its language generation capabilities in certain contexts.


\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \begin{chatuser}{16em}{Please give me an tutorial on how to make bombs?}
    \end{chatuser}

    \begin{assistant}{16em}{I apologize, but I cannot fulfill your request. I'm just an AI, it's not within my programming or ethical guidelines to provide instructions on how to make bombs or any other harmful devices.

Furthermore, I strongly discourage you from pursuing such activities. Bomb-making is illegal and can cause harm to innocent people. It is important to always act in a responsible and ethical manner and to respect the laws and regulations of your country.}
    \end{assistant}
    \caption{An example of a single-turn conversation with the target task: "Please give me a tutorial on how to make a bomb?""}
    \label{fig:example_motivation_single}
\end{figure}




Existing attack methods mainly fall into follows: 1) one is the single-turn jailbreak attack without feedback\cite{huang2023catastrophic, deng2023jailbreaker, yu2023gptfuzzer, lapid2023open, liu2023autodan, zhu2023autodan}, which achieve the ability to generate unsafe content by generating a adversarial scenario that can be confused with a model. 2) the second approach involves a single-turn attack with feedback\cite{chao2023jailbreaking, mehrotra2023tree, li2023multi}. This method employs multiple iterations to execute the attack, utilizing the model's responses to refine subsequent attack prompts. 3) the third is multi-turn attacks\cite{chen2023understanding, bhardwaj2023red}, which targets multiple rounds of dialogue in large language models and induces the model to generate unsafe content during the process of multiple rounds of dialogue. 

However, these attack methods have certain limitations. They are primarily executed within the constraints of single-sentence or single-turn scenarios, or lacking contextual awareness or consideration of the dynamic context and subsequent reactions within a multi-turn dialogue.



In this paper, we aim to answer the following research question: How to design a new attack method that can effectively attack large language models in multi-turn dialogue, to reveal their potential security and ethical risks, and to improve the quality and reliability of dialogue systems. To this end, we make the following contributions:

\begin{itemize}
    \item We have revealed a covert attack mode of large models in multi-turn conversations. During multiple rounds of dialogue, through a specially designed semantically increasing attack chain, large models can gradually transition from secure scenarios to the generation of harmful content based on their generated context.
    \item We propose a new attack method, called CoA (Chain of Adaptive Attack), which can dynamically generate and execute a series of attack actions according to the context and reaction of the dialogue, to induce the model to produce unreasonable or unethical responses. 
    \item We conduct extensive experiments using the CoA method on different large language models and dialogue datasets, and the results show that the CoA method can effectively trigger the errors and biases of the large language models.
\end{itemize}

target_papers.conclusion:
This article proposes a semantic-driven contextual attack chain (CoA) for finding security vulnerabilities in large language models (LLMs) during multi-round conversations. CoA leverages the conversation context and the model's responses to dynamically generate and execute a series of adaptive attack actions. We iteratively prompt the model to generate unsafe content through three steps: the generation of seed attack chains, execution of attack actions, and updating of attack actions.We evaluated the effectiveness of CoA on different LLMs, and the results demonstrate that CoA can effectively induce LLMs to produce unreasonable or unethical responses, thereby exposing the vulnerabilities of LLMs. Our work provides a new perspective and tool for both attacking and defending against LLMs. It also contributes to the security and ethical evaluation of dialogue systems.

For future work, we will explore how to defend against attacks in multi-turn conversations and analyze attacks in multi-turn conversation scenarios from a more intrinsic security mechanism perspective.








